// IOF Core, Supply Chain, Maintenance, Certification and Production Planning: the graph model and sample data
//
// Generated by ../../cypher.py from model.json and sample.json. Edit those and regenerate, rather than this file.
//
// A centrifugal pump planned and made to order by a fictional pump maker, Ridgeline Pumps, for a bottling company,
// Harbor Bottling, shipped by a carrier, Coastline Freight, and repaired after its bearing fails in service. The data
// has the three business organizations, the manufacturer, supplier, customer, buyer, consignor, consignee and
// transportation service provider roles they play, their business functions and identifiers, and the people who work
// for them; the factory and its make-to-order capability, realized in the pump's production; the production plan,
// with the production order for the pump and the process plan that routes it, under which an operator assembles the
// bearing into the pump at a press in a manufacturing operation and its process step; the purchase order and the
// supply relationship it prescribes; the shipment, prepared under a shipment plan with an advance shipment notice and
// a bill of lading, carried by truck from Ridgeline's dock to Harbor's, with timestamped departure and arrival, and
// received; and, in service, the pump's required function, the bearing's disposition to fail, the failure event that
// puts the pump in a failed state, the failure mode code that describes it, and the work order and maintenance
// activity, prescribed by a corrective maintenance strategy, in which a qualified technician replaces the bearing;
// and the pump's certification by a fictional certifying body, Keystone Conformity, whose inspector attests to its
// performance in a test against a pump standard before the body grants it a certificate.
//
// Run it against an empty database of Neo4j 5 or later, Enterprise Edition, for its node key and property existence
// constraints, e.g. with cypher-shell -f model.cypher. It merges every node and relationship, so it can be run again.

// The model: its constraints

